- So we stay away from
women, rednecks, and old men.

00:10:36.305 --> 00:10:39.310
- So they are planning
what's called voir dire,

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.220
which is the system by which you choose

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.950
the members of the jury that
will form the jury at trial.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:46.730
You have a large panel.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.810
Many of you have probably
served on jury duty,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.457
so you've seen this process in action.

00:10:50.457 --> 00:10:52.048
You start with a large panel

00:10:52.048 --> 00:10:54.650
and then you whittle that
down for various reasons.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.660
You are allowed to have what
are called peremptory strikes,

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.900
which gives you the
ability to strike a juror

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:01.480
for any reason whatsoever

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:03.650
and you're also given
strikes that allow you

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:05.570
to remove a member of the jury

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.725
or a potential member
of the jury for a cause.

00:11:08.725 --> 00:11:10.810
You demonstrate to the judge

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:13.370
that they are so prejudicial
that they cannot issue

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.860
a fair verdict.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.560
Often, the judge will
use his or her discretion

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.125
to remove those people from
the jury panel themselves

00:11:21.125 --> 00:11:24.596
and neither the prosecutor nor
the criminal defense attorney

00:11:24.596 --> 00:11:27.570
have to use a strike to
get rid of that person.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.880
But with peremptory
strikes, you are allowed,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.780
technically, to remove people
for any reason whatsoever.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.310
There are limits to that power

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:36.647
but practically speaking,

00:11:36.647 --> 00:11:39.350
that's what you do to try and get a jury

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:42.950
that is favorable to your side
as opposed to the other side.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.410
Of course, both the prosection and defense

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.080
are both using the same tools,

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.920
the peremptory strikes to try

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.523
and get the jury that they want.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.320
- Citizens, thank you.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.550
The council each have 12
challenges let's begin.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.763
- 12 challenges would
be peremptory strikes.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:04.137
- Your honor, the state
challenges jurors number six, 10.

00:12:07.002 --> 00:12:07.835
- Oh geeze.

00:12:07.835 --> 00:12:09.740
Okay what the movie is hinting at here

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:12.060
is that the prosector,
played by Kevin Spacey,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:16.960
is striking all of the African
Americans from the jury.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.180
You're not allowed to do that.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.930
Now its true, theoretically speaking,

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:22.030
that you can use a peremptory challenge

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:23.540
to get rid of anyone that you want.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.440
However, there are
constitutional limits to that.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.150
In 1986, the US supreme court,

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.340
in the case of Batson V. Kentucky,

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.970
held that a prosecutor
cannot use his or her

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:36.960
peremptory strikes to
strike all of the members

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.390
of one race from a jury

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.924
without stating a valid reason for each

00:12:41.924 --> 00:12:44.210
of the individual members who
were struck from that jury.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.990
That's exactly what Kevin
Spacey is doing here.

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:48.610
He said so earlier in the movie

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.270
that he was going to do that

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.270
and here he's trying to strike

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:54.410
all of the African
Americans from the jury.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.740
That is constitutionally infirm
under the sixth amendment

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:59.550
under the Batson v. Kentucky case

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:02.053
and I think that's a slam dunk on appeal.

00:13:03.188 --> 00:13:06.260
- I've totally reviewed your brief request

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:08.830
on change of venue

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:09.840
and I agree with you,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:13.260
I think its impossible to
find a fair and impartial jury

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:14.093
here in Canton.

00:13:14.093 --> 00:13:16.590
In fact, I think its
impossible to find a fair

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.190
and impartial jury
anywhere in Mississippi,

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:23.880
and as such, a jury here would be as fair

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:25.970
as a jury anywhere else.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:26.803
- Oh boy.

00:13:26.803 --> 00:13:28.550
- I've decided to deny your request

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.350
for a change of venue.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.470
- I think the judge
here is probably wrong.

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:35.870
Doesn't seem like he's really
considered the evidence

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:38.160
or the situation at bar.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.380
The city is at a boiling point.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.392
There are Ku Klux Klan
members protesting every day.

00:13:44.392 --> 00:13:47.300
That seems really really prejudicial

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.380
and there are probably parts of the state

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:52.890
where you can move this case
and get a more impartial jury

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.490
that hasn't heard as much as
everyone in this community has

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.222
so on top of the entire
conversation being improper,

00:13:59.222 --> 00:14:02.190
I think the decision
that the judge has made

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.280
is wrong, whether that
rises to what's called

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:06.470
an abuse of discretion,

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:08.810
which is generally the standard on appeal

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:12.110
when appeals court is reviewing
the lower courts decision.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:15.730
I don't know but the
judge is just wrong here

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:17.030
and given the impropriety,

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:19.334
its probably going to be overturned.

00:14:19.334 --> 00:14:22.084
(crowd shouting)

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.880
I'm gonna go out on a
limb and say when 100's

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.052
of Ku Klux Klan members
show up to your trial,

00:14:31.052 --> 00:14:33.760
its probably a good
idea to grant the motion

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.350
for change of venue.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:36.183
- Sheriff, if you would,

00:14:36.183 --> 00:14:39.493
would you please identify this weapon?

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.670
- The weapon found at the scene.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.050
- Were you able to identify
any of the fingerprints

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:46.140
found on this gun?

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.010
- Yes, the prints on the gun matched those

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.370
of Carl Lee Hailey.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:51.765
- You're sure?

00:14:51.765 --> 00:14:53.490
- Yes.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.680
- Your honor, I'd like to
enter this into evidence

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.260
as exhibit S18.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:00.370
We have no further use of this witness.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.649
- A couple of problems of
what just happened here

00:15:02.649 --> 00:15:04.550
talking about the murder weapon

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:05.870
in this criminal proceeding.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:08.170
Number one, again this
testimony may not be relevant

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:10.140
because I'm not sure it
goes to the state of mind

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.150
of the defendant.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:14.150
Number two, if you were
handling a physical object

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.220
where fingerprints where important,

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:19.330
it would be wrapped in plastic
in a sealed plastic bag.

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.980
You wouldn't be handling
it yourself in court.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:24.879
Also, you would probably
not want to bring an M16

00:15:24.879 --> 00:15:29.270
into the actual courtroom with
a magazine attached to it.

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:32.260
You can just use pictures and
enter those into evidence.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.630
On top of that, what the
prosecutor really should

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.060
be doing here is not just
identifying that this weapon

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.880
was used to kill the two victims

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.920
but what is relevant to state of mind

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.920
is the fact that Samuel L.
Jackson had to get that weapon

00:15:49.291 --> 00:15:52.370
out of his gun safe and he
had to load the magazine

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.450
and he had to put the
magazine into the gun.

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:58.040
He had to then sneak into
the courthouse, hide,

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.356
all of these things show premeditation

00:16:00.356 --> 00:16:02.556
and cognizance of what's going on

00:16:02.556 --> 00:16:05.660
to counteract the defense of insanity.

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:08.040
That is what this prosector
should be showing.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:11.560
That the defendant knew what he was doing

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.160
and isn't legally insane.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.884
Its not just that he used
a gun to kill people.

00:16:15.884 --> 00:16:18.610
That actually supports
the defendants side.

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:20.300
What he needs to do is negate that

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.100
and talk about his state of mind

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.710
or at least show the
things that demonstrate

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:26.080
the state of mind of the defendant.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:28.430
He's not doing that and as a result,

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.040
he is not preventing
the defense from using

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:32.790
their insanity plea.

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:35.040
- Did you arrest Billy
Ray Cobb and Pete Willard?

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.080
- Yes, I did.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:40.021
I arrested them for the
rape and attempted murder

00:16:40.021 --> 00:16:42.850
of 10 year old Tonya Hailey.

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:45.994
- Is it true that Pete Willard
signed a written confession

00:16:45.994 --> 00:16:50.490
saying that he and Billy Ray
Cobb did rape Tonya Hailey?

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.220
- Objection your honor,
that's inadmissible

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:53.840
and Mr. Brigance knows it.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:56.510
- Mr. Brigance, I've spoken to you before.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.560
We are not trying the
rape of Ms. Hailey today

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:01.990
but the murder of two young men

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.091
and if you continue in this performance

00:17:04.091 --> 00:17:06.710
I'll hold you in contempt.

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:09.800
- Okay, the prosector was way way late

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:11.860
in objecting if that is inadmissible

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:14.010
then he should of objected to the fact

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.260
that the policemen arrested
the two men before.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.112
Its not just the confession

00:17:19.112 --> 00:17:22.250
but all of the evidence of bad acts

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.534
that would be inadmissible.

00:17:24.534 --> 00:17:26.119
But as I pointed out before,

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:27.920
number one I think it is admissible

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.323
because it goes to the state
of mind of the defendant.

00:17:30.323 --> 00:17:32.800
What he thought those people did

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:34.693
and again, ironically, its the prosection

00:17:34.693 --> 00:17:36.850
that would want that information in.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:40.610
It shows that the defendant
acted in a premeditated way

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:44.760
because of the sexual
assault of his daughter.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.780
So I am skeptical that number one,

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:48.400
that information is inadmissible

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:50.610
and number two, its the prosecution

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:53.506
that would want that in in this courtroom.

00:17:53.506 --> 00:17:56.940
So some weird perverse arguing here.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.747
- My name is Dr. Wilbert Rodeheaver

00:17:59.747 --> 00:18:02.680
the chair of psychiatry of
Mississippi state University

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:05.520
and clinical director of
the Whitfield facility

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:07.113
for the criminally insane.

00:18:08.167 --> 00:18:11.476
- Thank you doctor, your
credentials speak for themselves.

00:18:11.476 --> 00:18:12.730
- No they don't.

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.750
Okay when you are trying
to use expert testimony

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.530
on the stand it is very
very important to bolster

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:19.890
that witness credibility.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.420
Often you will spend
half an hour, 45 minutes,

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:25.010
an hour just going over the credentials

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.010
of your witness, to demonstrate

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.550
that your witness is entirely credible

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:32.200
and that their opinion is based on science

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:33.707
and experience.

00:18:33.707 --> 00:18:35.940
In the federal court
systems you go through

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:38.320
what's called the Daubert Standard

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.620
for ensuring that your witness

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.000
meets the standard for admissible evidence

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:44.370
and being able to opine

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:46.780
because regular witness, lay witnesses,

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:48.610
are not allowed to offer their opinion.

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:50.440
They are only allowed to talk about

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.420
what they actually saw themselves

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:54.870
and what's within their
own personal experience.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:55.988
Experts, on the other hand,

00:18:55.988 --> 00:18:58.130
are allowed to offer their opinion.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:18:59.830
That is what they are there to do

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.320
is to give their scientific opinion

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.410
based on the evidence
that you put before them.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:07.000
So it is incredibly important

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.940
to make sure that not only you demonstrate

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.880
the foundation that your
witness, your expert witness,

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:13.990
knows what they're talking about

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:16.590
but that their credentials
really are impeccable

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:19.810
and simply stating that
your expert psychologist

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:21.526
is in fact a psychologist

00:19:21.526 --> 00:19:23.170
is not gonna get it done.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:24.310
Number one, that doesn't demonstrate

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.530
to the judge that their opinion is valid

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:28.060
and number two, it doesn't show

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:30.950
to the jury that they
should trust this person

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:32.900
and as this movie has pointed out,

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:36.930
credibility of an expert
opinion is very very important.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:39.130
- Now, doctor, can you please explain,

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:41.380
as you do so eloquently in your book,

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.780
the M'Naughten rule for the jury?

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:46.460
- Certainly, the M'Naughten rule states

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:50.323
that a person must be unable
to tell right from wrong

00:19:50.323 --> 00:19:54.833
and or be unaware of the
consequences of his actions

00:19:54.833 --> 00:19:57.690
to be termed legally insane.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:01.430
- Okay that is actually a
reasonably good statement

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.200
about what the M'Naughten rule is.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:05.930
Now a couple problems with that.

00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:07.990
Number one, that's a legal standard

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:11.150
so its not really up to
the expert psychologist

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:13.702
to opine as to the ultimate issue

00:20:13.702 --> 00:20:17.200
about whether the defendant
meets the legal standard

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.300
for legal insanity.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:22.158
That's for the lawyers to
do based on the witnesses

00:20:22.158 --> 00:20:26.326
testimony about their opinion
about the mental state.

00:20:26.326 --> 00:20:29.490
The bigger problem is that no one has used

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:33.660
the M'Naughten rule in 60 years.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:36.270
The M'Naghten rule actually is based on

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:39.440
English common law in the 1850s

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.980
where someone attempted to kill
the British prime minister,

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.529
well M'Naughten tried to kill
the British prime minister.

00:20:45.529 --> 00:20:50.529
He missed and killed the
prime minister's secretary.

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:52.860
A man named Peale, I believe.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.380
Now based on the circumstances,

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:55.769
I think it was the court of Exchequer

00:20:55.769 --> 00:20:58.830
said that M'Naughten was legally insane

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:01.090
and could not be found guilty as a result.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:05.960
He was then put in a insane
asylum for the rest of his life.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:09.950
Most US courts replaced the
M'Naughten rule in the 1950s

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.030
with what's called the Durham rule

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:14.720
and at least regarding
federal courts in the 1980s,

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.610
the Durham rule was
replaced with a definition

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:21.044
of insanity laid out in the
Comprehensive Crime Control Act

00:21:21.044 --> 00:21:23.140
of 1984.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.400
So the M'Naughten rule hasn't been used

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:28.460
in a very very long time.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.350
In most jurisdictions now,

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:33.210
the defense has to prove insanity by clear

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:34.240
and convincing evidence

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:35.617
which is a very high standard.

00:21:35.617 --> 00:21:38.620
Its not quite as high as
beyond a reasonable doubt

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:40.400
but its still a very high burden.

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.563
To demonstrate that the
defendant at the time

00:21:42.563 --> 00:21:46.230
of the alleged wrongdoing
did not understand

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:49.042
the nature or quality
of his or her actions

00:21:49.042 --> 00:21:53.210
and did not understand the
wrongfulness of the act.

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:56.130
So its a much higher
standard than that laid

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:57.476
out in M'Naughten.

00:21:57.476 --> 00:21:59.810
- Doctor, who do you work for?

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.741
- State of Mississippi.

00:22:01.741 --> 00:22:03.130
- In your 11 years of
working for the state,

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.020
how many times have
you testified in trials

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:06.680
where the insanity defense was used?

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.160
- This is my 46th trial.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:10.053
- 46th trial.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.577
Well of those 46 trials, how many times

00:22:12.577 --> 00:22:16.740
did you testify that the
defendant was legally insane?

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:17.780
- Objection, your honor,

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:19.270
the good doctor cannot be asked

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.460
to recall all of his testimony
at these previous trials.

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:23.700
Its absurd.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.740
- Overruled.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:26.700
- Yeah, that's a really bad objection.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:28.680
One of the most common questions

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:30.286
you will get as an expert witness

00:22:30.286 --> 00:22:32.602
is how many times you've testified,

00:22:32.602 --> 00:22:35.010
how many times you've
testified for one side

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.410
versus you've testified on the other

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:40.140
because people know that there are experts

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:43.960
that are going to testify
on behalf of the defendants

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:46.726
or largely on behalf of the prosecution.

00:22:46.726 --> 00:22:50.700
Often, a good witness will
intentionally have cases

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.690
where they have testified on either side,

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:55.195
purposefully to avoid seeming biased

00:22:55.195 --> 00:22:56.950
in this kind of context

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:59.580
where you have only testified for one side

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:00.720
instead of the other.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.150
- I can't remember.

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:04.176
- Could it be, doctor, that
the reason you can't remember

00:23:04.176 --> 00:23:07.608
is that in 11 years and 46 trials

00:23:07.608 --> 00:23:09.770
you never have seen a defendant

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:11.770
whom you have found insane.

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:13.882
- I can't recall at this time.

00:23:13.882 --> 00:23:16.360
- That's another way to
screw up your testimony

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.050
is to say that you know definitively

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:20.630
that you've testified 46 times

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.820
but you can't remember
how many times you voted

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:25.021
on the other side.

00:23:25.021 --> 00:23:27.920
That's opening yourself
up cross examination

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.920
and destroying your credibility
as an expert witness.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:32.820
You don't do stupid things like that.

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:34.390
- You never have seen a defendant

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:36.380
whom you have found insane.

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.053
- I can't recall at this time.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.520
- Can you recall testifying
at the trail of one Dan Baker?

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:44.560
- Objection, your honor, this has nothing

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:46.350
to do with the proceedings here.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.690
- Overruled, this better
be good, Mr. Brigance.

00:23:51.335 --> 00:23:52.243
- Thank you, your honor.

00:23:52.243 --> 00:23:55.950
- This better be good is
not a reason to overrule

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:57.880
someone's objection.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.170
The prosecutor should
have stated his grounds

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:03.270
for objecting and gotten a response

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:04.630
from Matthew McCognaughey

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:07.210
but yeah there is no legal
standard that evidence

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:09.540
can come in if its going to be good.

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:11.260
Doesn't work that way.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:15.594
- In 1985, you testified that
Dan Baker was legally sane.

00:24:15.594 --> 00:24:19.570
The jury disagreed with you and Mr. Baker

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:22.079
was found not guilty
by reason of insanity.

00:24:22.079 --> 00:24:25.150
Since that time, he has been
a patient in your hospital

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:27.190
under your care as a
paranoid schizophrenic.

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:28.710
Is that correct, doctor?

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:30.040
- Yeah.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:33.230
- Do you normally admit
patients, keep and treat them

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:35.810
for 10 years if they are
of sound mind and body?

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:37.070
- Of course not.

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:38.673
- Then it would be fair to say

00:24:38.673 --> 00:24:43.520
that you find insane people
sane for the purposes of trial.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:46.060
- Objective your honor,
this is argumentative.

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:47.810
- Yeah, so that is argumentative

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:51.060
but the points that he was
making were perfectly valid

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:54.780
points and as a good way to show the bias

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.170
of a particular expert in this case.

00:24:57.170 --> 00:24:58.710
Matthew McConaughey has done a good job

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:00.600
of impeaching this witness credibility

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.680
by showing that he testified
previously that a man

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:07.240
was legally sane but
then admitted that person

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:10.550
to his mental institution
as an insane person

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:13.196
and has kept him there for over 10 years

00:25:13.196 --> 00:25:15.150
on the basis that he's insane

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:17.730
and that actually raises
a really interesting part

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.010
of the insanity defense.

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:21.725
Most people get really
upset when they think about

00:25:21.725 --> 00:25:26.680
a murderer being found not
guilty by reason of insanity

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:28.270
thinking that they just go free.

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:29.260
They don't.

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:31.860
When you find someone legally insane,

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.740
they go to a mental institution.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:35.860
They do not go into the public

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:38.699
and its true that there is no set time

00:25:38.699 --> 00:25:42.140
that they have to be kept
in that mental institution

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:43.842
but that really cuts both ways.

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:47.410
Its true that they can
be let out immediately

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:50.300
upon finding that they are legally sane

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:51.800
but what happens most of the time

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:55.120
is those people are kept in that place

00:25:55.120 --> 00:25:56.540
for the rest of their lives

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:59.090
and in fact, M'Naughten who gave the name

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:00.390
to the M'Naughten rule

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:03.740
after he was found not
guilty by reason of insanity

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:05.540
spent the rest of his life

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:07.750
in a mental institution.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:10.110
So don't think that just because someone

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.320
is found not guilty by reason of insanity

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:13.930
it means that they're going scot free

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:15.310
into the public at large,

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:17.530
on the contrary, they might spend

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:20.550
more time in a mental
institution then they would

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:23.640
if they were found guilty
of the underlining crime.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:24.663
- Mr. Hailey.

00:26:26.224 --> 00:26:28.360
Before you stepped outside of yourself

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:30.839
to watch yourself shoot
Mr. Willard and Mr. Cobb,

00:26:30.839 --> 00:26:35.040
were you aware that if
convicted they might be freed

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.290
in only 10 years?

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.780
- Yes, I heard people say that, yes.

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:41.740
- Okay, goes to his mental state.

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:42.573
- No.

00:26:42.573 --> 00:26:43.890
- Do you think two men who rape a child

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:45.370
should be free in 10 years?

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:46.613
- Its starting to get argumentative.

00:26:46.613 --> 00:26:48.040
- Hang a child.
- Object.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:49.450
- Should be free in 10 years?

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:50.283
- Object.

00:26:50.283 --> 00:26:51.116
- Well what do you think
should happen to them,

00:26:51.116 --> 00:26:52.130
what would be a fair sentence?

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:53.220
- Object.

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:54.150
- Do you think they
deserved to die, Mr. Hailey.

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:55.731
- Your honor.
- Now, Mr. Buckley.

00:26:55.731 --> 00:26:56.666
- Carl, don't answer that question.

00:26:56.666 --> 00:26:57.499
- They deserved to die?

00:26:57.499 --> 00:26:58.332
- Yes they deserved to die

00:26:58.332 --> 00:27:00.740
and I hope they burn in hell!

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.530
- Yeah, so this is such an obvious trope

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:04.914
in legal dramas.

00:27:04.914 --> 00:27:06.500
The prosecutor doesn't get to do that.

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.550
You don't get to slowly
approach the witness

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:12.300
as your asking more
argumentative questions

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:13.868
after argumentative question.

00:27:13.868 --> 00:27:16.560
Number one, you don't get
to enter the well like that

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:17.670
without permission of the judge.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.180
The bailiff will tackle you.

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:20.800
On top of that, you don't
get to raise your voice

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:24.180
and yell at the defendant
and get this Perry Mason

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:27.339
moment where the defendant
basically admits that

00:27:27.339 --> 00:27:30.550
he wanted to pre
meditatively kill someone.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:33.330
Like, that's, that's not
how these things work.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.210
The defendant has a 5th amendment right

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:36.300
not to take the stand

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:39.190
and there is nothing in that
testimony that he just said

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:40.627
that helped his case and in fact,

00:27:40.627 --> 00:27:43.170
I think legally speaking, he showed

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:46.560
that he had the mental
state required for murder

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:49.610
and not the mental state required
for the insanity defense.

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:52.780
So that's a classic
reason why you don't put

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:54.370
a criminal defendant up on the stand.

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:56.160
There's just no reason for it.

00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:57.363
- Soaked in her blood.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:27:59.943
Left to die.

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:04.847
Can you see her?

00:28:07.364 --> 00:28:08.414
I want you to picture

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:12.083
a little girl.

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:19.953
Now imagine she's white.

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:25.670
- Okay that is a very
eloquent closing argument.

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.790
The problem is that in closing arguments

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:31.410
you are not allowed to
put improper emphasis

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:33.650
on the victim's race.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:35.850
Historically it has been the prosecution

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:40.300
who has put too much emphasis on the race

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:44.440
of the victim in order
to drum up high emotions

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.720
against the defendant
but I think the same rule

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:48.080
applies to the defense.

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:50.089
The system is supposed to be colorblind

00:28:50.089 --> 00:28:53.080
and the race of the victim here

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:56.428
is not strictly relevant
as to whether or not

00:28:56.428 --> 00:28:58.576
the defendant in this particularity case

00:28:58.576 --> 00:29:01.980
is more likely or not to
have committed the crime

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:03.522
that he's accused of.

00:29:03.522 --> 00:29:06.790
So I think that that's
probably an improper closing

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:07.623
argument to make

00:29:07.623 --> 00:29:12.137
and it is likely to be an issue on appeal.

00:29:12.137 --> 00:29:14.720
(upbeat music)

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:19.630
Okay now it is time to
give A Time to Kill a grade

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.134
for legal realism.

00:29:21.134 --> 00:29:22.905
(gavel pounding)

00:29:22.905 --> 00:29:26.010
This was a heavier movie than we are used

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:28.050
to dealing with on this series

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:31.032
but I think its important
to address these dramas

00:29:31.032 --> 00:29:33.203
because these kind of issues do show up

00:29:33.203 --> 00:29:36.240
in real life courtrooms from time to time

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:38.420
and I would be remiss not

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:40.940
to talk about these subjects

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:42.480
and I think this movie does a good job

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:45.040
of talking about some issues
that you don't really see.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:48.760
The economics of being
a solo practitioner,

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:52.160
the issues of race that show up constantly

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:54.430
in our judicial system,
regardless of our efforts

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:55.836
to try and stamp those outs

00:29:55.836 --> 00:29:58.350
and even some technical things like

00:29:58.350 --> 00:29:59.290
the M'Naughten rule,

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:01.720
while its not the right standard,

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.552
it was the right standard
at a certain point in time

00:30:04.552 --> 00:30:07.560
and they did deal with
some of the nuance there.

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:09.300
Unfortunately, the movie dealt

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.100
a lot with over dramatizations

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:14.630
they left out a lot of
the proceedings that would

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:17.250
normally occur and often oddly

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:21.560
the prosecution was
arguing 100% the wrong way.

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:23.530
They were arguing for
things that would help

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.400
the defense and the
defense was arguing things

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:26.475
that would help the prosecution.

00:30:26.475 --> 00:30:30.730
So all in all, I give A
Time to Kill a B minus.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:33.180
It is definitely a
worthwhile drama to watch

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:36.110
but some of the nuance is plainly lost.

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:38.060
A Time to Kill shows us what happens

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:40.657
to our institutions when
they are at a breaking point.

00:30:40.657 --> 00:30:42.480
I'm currently enjoying a documentary

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:44.770
on how that kind of stress
effects the presidency

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:46.060
on Curiosity Stream.

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:47.810
Its called Presidents in Crisis.

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:49.770
Its a great piece about
how presidents Lincoln,

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.035
Roosevelt, and Johnson
were all transformed

00:30:52.035 --> 00:30:54.530
by personal trauma and how that affected

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:55.877
the way that they governed.

00:30:55.877 --> 00:30:58.270
If you're looking for
the best documentaries

00:30:58.270 --> 00:30:59.950
and nonfiction movies out there,

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:02.193
you should definitely
check out curiosity stream.

